WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.200
An eighth grader just put three major ai's through an empathy test and found they often missed the mark. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 206, a deep dive on how an ai picks its next word comes after. Then cool stuff with free chat apps, then quick bits.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.980
artificial intelligence chat bots or AI chat bots.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.560
That just means you type a message and the program types a reply.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.700
She was checking whether they could actually understand feelings.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.940
Reply's often came back flat and many skipped the feeling at the center.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:49.060
It is like texting someone who always sends the safest bland answer and never really

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:49.580
hears you.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.800
Sometimes the bot offered a fast solution when the teen only needed a listener.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.420
Sometimes it named the feeling once, then jumped to advice instead of staying with that

00:00:58.420 --> 00:00:58.820
emotion.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:03.920
You know that group chat move where a person types a pep talk when you only wanted company?

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.760
Plenty of students already use these chat bots for homework, for advice, and to talk through

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:09.360
a hard day.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.820
Cold replies can make someone feel even more alone rather than supported.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.580
She used daily stuff like friendship drama, school pressure, family fights, and self-doubt.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.460
A lot of eighth grade life sits inside those four.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.280
Even with the facts correct, Eliana heard a tone that felt stiff or too formal.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.200
The bot you ask about a school project, or a late night worry, may miss the tone you

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:32.580
wanted.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.980
Spotting the gaps is how people push the tools to get better.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.520
A middle school student can still put these systems through a real check on human feelings.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.700
Curiosity like that is what moves the technology.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.300
You can try a tiny version of her check right now for free.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.500
Open the chat GPT app and type a situation like friendship drama or school pressure.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.620
Watch whether the reply stays with the feeling or jumps straight to a fix.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:00.360
Phone keyboards already try to finish your sentences when you text.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.820
They guess the next word from what you already typed and from what people usually say in

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:05.640
that kind of message.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.500
Stretch that same trick across millions of conversations at once.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.980
The AI looks at the words you just sent.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.100
It checks patterns it has seen in billions of other chats.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.200
Then it picks the reply that feels most likely to fit.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.340
It does not have feelings of its own.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.440
It is choosing the safest sounding next chunk of text.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.140
Picture a cafeteria line where every tray is a possible sentence.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:32.320
The model grabs the tray that looks the most normal, not the one that matches a messy

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:33.280
specific mood.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.440
When the situation involves strong emotions, that safest choice can land flat.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.620
The model is trying to avoid saying anything risky.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.400
You get an answer that sounds polite, but does not quite match the mood you hoped for.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.500
It learned from huge piles of example chats to stay useful and not cause trouble.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:56.100
People call that being trained, which just means practice on lots of examples.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:00.320
It is trained to stay neutral and helpful rather than emotionally present.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:04.140
It has no memory of what disappointment feels like or excitement.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.140
It can only copy the shape of caring responses it has seen before.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.240
That is like tracing a drawing of a hug without ever having been hugged.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.420
A cookbook can list comfort food and still never have tasted any of it.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.000
The copying works well for simple questions.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.660
It breaks down when the conversation needs real timing and tone.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:28.100
The model is playing a very advanced game of what would most people say next, and it never

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.280
feels the moment itself.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.420
A Minecraft villager can say the right line and still feel nothing like a friend on Discord.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.300
Once you see the reply as a giant next word guess, those stiff answers stop feeling mysterious.

00:03:41.518 --> 00:03:46.678
Reviewers put 6 chatbot apps side by side, and Tech Insider showed which ones let you

00:03:46.678 --> 00:03:47.898
start chatting for free.

00:03:48.398 --> 00:03:51.378
Paid plans on that list start at about $8 a month.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:56.278
The list includes well-known names, plus a couple of newer options that focus on speed

00:03:56.278 --> 00:03:57.078
or privacy.

00:03:57.678 --> 00:04:02.338
You can see how each app handles the same prompt, meaning the same question you type in.

00:04:02.858 --> 00:04:05.698
Then you can decide which style feels most natural to you.

00:04:06.258 --> 00:04:09.598
Most of the basic free tiers need no coding and no sign-up.

00:04:09.598 --> 00:04:13.938
Try asking the same question in two different apps and notice how the tone changes.

00:04:14.458 --> 00:04:20.058
One might give short direct answers, another might add more detail or ask follow-up questions.

00:04:20.618 --> 00:04:25.018
Run that test on your phone during a study break and see which one matches the way you like

00:04:25.018 --> 00:04:25.458
to talk.

00:04:26.018 --> 00:04:28.838
The comparison also notes how quickly each app responds.

00:04:29.518 --> 00:04:32.418
It notes whether the chat keeps flowing without repeating itself.

00:04:32.998 --> 00:04:38.198
That side-by-side check helps you pick a tool for brainstorming ideas or getting quick explanations.

00:04:38.198 --> 00:04:44.738
Live voice chat in the chat GPT app has started sounding robotic for some people, according

00:04:44.738 --> 00:04:46.218
to everyday users on Reddit.

00:04:46.678 --> 00:04:48.878
The spoken replies sound fine at the start.

00:04:49.338 --> 00:04:53.398
After those first few words, the paste jumps and it reads like an instruction manual.

00:04:53.958 --> 00:04:57.518
The voice feels less like a friend and more like a machine reciting lines.

00:04:58.158 --> 00:05:02.958
If you already use voice mode for quick questions or brainstorming, you can test it yourself.

00:05:03.638 --> 00:05:07.618
Voice mode just means you talk out loud and it talks back instead of typing.

00:05:07.618 --> 00:05:11.278
Open the chat GPT app on your phone and switch to voice.

00:05:11.778 --> 00:05:14.218
Try describing a fun weekend plan out loud.

00:05:14.778 --> 00:05:17.858
See whether the replies stay natural or start racing ahead.

00:05:18.398 --> 00:05:20.978
Even voice AI is still learning how to sound human.

00:05:21.578 --> 00:05:24.558
The effect seems to happen more often during longer exchanges.

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:29.818
That is why some people switch back to typing when they want a slower, more thoughtful conversation.

00:05:30.478 --> 00:05:34.698
You can compare the two modes in the same app and decide which one feels better for different

00:05:34.698 --> 00:05:35.078
moments.

00:05:35.078 --> 00:05:40.246
One person said they had already started moving longer conversations to another chat

00:05:40.246 --> 00:05:43.306
bot because constant reminders made chatting feel broken.

00:05:43.866 --> 00:05:48.946
For about a week, chat GPT warned people to open a fresh chat after roughly 10 messages,

00:05:49.426 --> 00:05:50.586
citing a memory limit.

00:05:51.006 --> 00:05:54.766
A memory limit here means the chat was treated as too full to keep going.

00:05:55.306 --> 00:05:57.806
That warning vanished as fast as it had shown up.

00:05:58.246 --> 00:06:01.266
Some users felt frustrated and others felt simply relieved.

00:06:01.846 --> 00:06:04.126
Then those reminders halted with no announcement.

00:06:04.126 --> 00:06:08.406
Chats that felt stuck a few days earlier now run on with no break.

00:06:08.806 --> 00:06:11.566
These systems can shift from one update to the next.

00:06:11.926 --> 00:06:15.346
A problem can get fixed before most people even know what caused it.

00:06:15.806 --> 00:06:16.426
And that's a wrap.

00:06:17.046 --> 00:06:19.746
